domain x>0, integration by parts gives x ln(x^3)-3x+C.\nq-50222: Taking u=x^2 and v=-cos(3x)/3 gives the selected reduction formula. Further integration yields -x^2 cos(3x)/3+2x sin(3x)/9+2cos(3x)/27+C, confirming the formula.\nq-114942: An antiderivative is x^3(3ln^2(x)-2ln(x)+2/3)/ln^2(2). Evaluation at e and 1 gives (5e^3-2)/(3ln^2(2)).\nq-114944: On [1,e], the integrand is 4ln^2(x)/ln^2(3). Its antiderivative is 4x(ln^2(x)-2ln(x)+2)/ln^2(3), giving (4e-8)/ln^2(3).\nq-77776: The transformation sends (0,-1) to (3,-2) and (1,3) to (-10,6). Their image line has slope -8/13 and equation 8x+13y+2=0.\nq-8309: Integration gives sin(2y)/2=-cos(3x)/3+C. The initial condition sets C=1/3, giving 3sin(2y)=2(1-cos(3x)). The stated y range selects y=arcsin(2(1-cos(3x))/3)/2, locally on the interval containing zero where the argument is less than 1.\nq-50220: For the nonzero solution, separation gives 1/y=x+C; y(1)=1 sets C=0, hence y=1/x on x>0. The source's intermediate claim of a general solution omits the equilibrium y=0, but this does not affect the requested particular solution.",
